Mild transient anterior epistaxis secondary to digital trauma (nose-picking)
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Unilateral anterior bleeding, Visible mucosal abrasion, History of digital trauma

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-026
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of spontaneous nasal bleeding requiring assessment to achieve hemostasis and exclude severe posterior epistaxis or systemic coagulopathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted active nasal bleeding that had not fully ceased with initial pressure. Urgent physical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to visualize the bleeding source, apply chemical cautery if needed, and confidently rule out posterior epistaxis which carries a high risk of airway compromise or severe hemorrhage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My nose started bleeding heavily out of nowhere and it keeps dripping even after I hold it.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports unilateral trickling of bright red blood from the anterior naris, which began suddenly. Admits to a direct reported history of recent digital mechanical trauma prior to onset. Notes rapid cessation of active bleeding with applied direct external pressure during the consultation, though a slow ooze persists. Denies swallowing large amounts of blood or coughing up blood. Complete absence of clinical easy bruising history.
Functional Status: Fully independent, slightly anxious.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding blood loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, sitting upright with tissue held to the nose.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals a visible superficial mucosal abrasion over Kiesselbach's plexus with a small, unstable clot. Diagnostic triad confirmed: unilateral anterior bleeding, visible mucosal abrasion, and history of digital trauma. Complete absence of posterior oropharyngeal blood flow visualized. Normal mucosal vascularity outside the specifically traumatized zone. Normal hemodynamic status without resting tachycardia. Lack of petechiae or purpura on generalized skin examination.
Environmental Observations: Tissues with moderate bright red blood present.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient is not taking any anticoagulants or antiplatelet agents.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild transient anterior epistaxis secondary to digital trauma (nose-picking) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Posterior Epistaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bleeding clearly visualized originating from the anterior septum, and absence of brisk bleeding down the posterior pharynx.

Coagulopathy/Bleeding Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of generalized petechiae, no history of easy bruising, and normal response to localized pressure.

Nasal Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear visualization of the anterior septal mucosa with no foreign material present.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with active nasal bleeding that failed initial home management. An urgent physical examination was mandated to determine the exact anatomical source of the bleeding and to ensure airway safety. Given that posterior bleeding can lead to rapid hemodynamic collapse or aspiration, delaying assessment was contraindicated. The face-to-face evaluation confirmed a benign anterior mucosal tear, effectively arresting the hemorrhage through targeted pressure and preventing an unnecessary ED presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Re-application of direct, continuous firm pressure to the cartilaginous lower nose for 10 full minutes. Application of topical petroleum jelly to the anterior septum to prevent mucosal drying and crusting.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a small amount of saline gel to the nasal septum twice daily using a cotton swab instead of a finger to maintain mucosal hydration and break the habit of digital trauma.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only if bleeding recurs frequently.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the bleeding restarts and does not stop after 20 minutes of continuous firm pressure, if you begin vomiting blood, or if you feel dizzy or lightheaded upon standing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a superficial mucosal injury; re-bleeding is common within the first 48 hours if the clot is dislodged.
Clinical Course: The bleeding will stop with sustained pressure, and the mucosal abrasion will heal over 5-7 days if undisturbed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you taste a constant flow of blood running down the back of your throat, or if bleeding occurs from both nostrils simultaneously and heavily.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A small blood vessel just inside the nose was scratched and bled. Holding pressure exactly over the soft part of the nose clamps the vessel shut so a clot can form.
Actionable Steps: Do not blow your nose, pick your nose, or bend over heavily for the next 24 hours. Sneeze with your mouth open. Apply pressure correctly if it restarts.

SMS INFORMATION

